const HTML = "<p>Suppose you start an organisation to help people. You spend your own evenings on it, ask friends for money and do work nobody is paying you to do, until eventually enough people support it that you can leave your job. Now helping people is your job, which seems an excellent arrangement. You can give it the attention it deserves.</p>\n<p>A salary doesn&#39;t corrupt the work, and neither does an office or someone to answer the phone; an organisation that relies on everybody being exhausted and unpaid has problems of its own. But the salary changes what a failure would mean. You might once have lost an argument about how best to help, whereas now you might lose the money you use to pay your mortgage.</p>\n<p>You hire others who believe in the work too, and a successful year allows you to offer them some security. You are pleased about this. Then a donor becomes difficult.</p>\n<p>Perhaps they want a project presented more favourably than its results justify. The work has helped some people, but fewer than you promised, and publishing the figures plainly might cost you their support. You could refuse the money, of course. You could also lose two members of staff and close the part of the project that is helping people, which makes the donor&#39;s request easier to consider once you have pictured those consequences.</p>\n<p>Nothing requires you to think of yourself as dishonest. You can think of yourself as responsible: the figures aren&#39;t being invented, the disappointing ones are simply receiving less attention, and there will be another report next year when things have improved. For now, the money stays and so do the jobs.</p>\n<p>That decision leaves you with a practical problem. Next year&#39;s figures will be compared with this year&#39;s report, so being candid then would expose how you presented them now. You have acquired a reason to keep doing something you originally intended to do once.</p>\n<p>I find this route to corruption more believable than the one in which a decent person wakes up wanting to be a villain. Each concession has something respectable to recommend it: loyalty to employees, the survival of useful work, a family that shouldn&#39;t suffer because its provider chose a helping profession. All of those concerns can be sincere without telling us whether the organisation is still doing enough good to justify what is being done in its name.</p>\n<p>The person asking that question may not be welcome. If they work there, colleagues can see them as a threat to their jobs; if they don&#39;t, they can be dismissed for not understanding how difficult the work is. Both answers may contain some truth. Neither answers the question.</p>\n<p>Imagine that the organisation has grown for several years while the number of people it helps has fallen, and its salaries, travel and fundraising costs have risen. Those costs might have an explanation; figures need examining, not waving about. But if the organisation&#39;s first response is to describe the dedication of its staff, it is changing the subject. People can work very hard at preserving something that no longer deserves to survive in its present form.</p>\n<p>The founder has the most to lose by admitting this. There is the income, but also the explanation of a life: years of work, introductions at parties, people who admire the commitment. Accepting that the organisation has gone wrong would require a more difficult account of what those years produced. It is tempting to defend the good person who began it, as though the present conduct were a misunderstanding that could be cleared up by describing the beginning.</p>\n<p>I don&#39;t think the beginning was necessarily a lie. Someone may have cared deeply and done valuable work, then later used both facts to excuse behaviour they would once have condemned. The people still praising their dedication help them avoid noticing the change.</p>\n<p>It is easier to recognise this in a charitable organisation than in whatever pays my own bills. I would want my circumstances considered and credit for what I had done well. If changing course meant making life harder for people who depended on me, I would take that seriously, and I would probably resent somebody presenting the choice as simple.</p>\n<p>But the people the organisation was created to help also have circumstances. They have been losing out while everyone employed by it discusses how much they stand to lose. At some point the founder has to answer for that, even if the answer threatens the work, the income and the account of themselves they have preferred until now.</p>\n";

